<BaseLayout
  title="Six-month vs. annual policies"
  description="How auto insurance policy terms work: six-month vs. annual, renewals, and mid-term changes."
>
  <a class="back-link" href="/learn/">&larr; All guides</a>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Six-month vs. annual policies</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Auto insurance is commonly sold as either a six-month or a twelve-month
      (annual) policy. Either way, you can often choose to pay monthly — but
      that's a billing choice, not the length of the policy itself.
    </p>

    <h2>Monthly payment isn't the same as a monthly policy</h2>
    <p>
      Paying "monthly" almost always means you're paying installments toward
      a six-month or annual policy, not buying insurance one month at a time.
      The policy term determines when your rate is locked in and when it can
      next change — the billing schedule is separate from that.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the term length matters</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Rate changes:</strong> your premium is generally fixed for
        the length of the term. A six-month policy gives the insurer (and
        you) more frequent chances to adjust the price; a twelve-month policy
        locks in longer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Renewal shopping:</strong> a shorter term means more frequent
        renewal dates, which are natural points to re-shop and compare other
        insurers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mid-term changes:</strong> adding a driver, a vehicle, or a
        ticket during the term can trigger a rate or coverage change before
        the term ends, regardless of whether it's a six-month or annual
        policy.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cancellation:</strong> how much you get back if you cancel
        early, and what notice your insurer must give you, are governed by
        state rules and your policy's own terms.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        <strong>Verified example — California:</strong> California requires
        that a six-month premium be no more than half of the equivalent
        annual premium, and that no separate "paid-in-full" discount be
        layered on top of that comparison. See
        <a href="/auto-insurance/ca/">California's verified auto insurance
        rules</a> for the source. This is a California-specific rule — it
        doesn't necessarily apply in other states, and we only show a rule
        like this once it's been verified from a primary source.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      When comparing a six-month quote to an annual quote from a different
      insurer, double the six-month number (or halve the annual number) so
      you're comparing like with like, and confirm what happens to the price
      at the next renewal rather than assuming it stays the same.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
